  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through early next week. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front will cross the forecast area early Friday morning,\nand will be mostly moisture starved. There is a chance for \nscattered rain showers along this boundary, however, rainfall \namounts are likely to remain below a tenth of an inch, with no \nimprovement to drought conditions. A second round of showers\nwill follow, mostly in the north, along a secondary front. Small\nbut non-zero CAPE of up to 200 J/kg along with NW flow 300 to \n320 degrees could result in some convective showers behind the\nsecondary front through Friday afternoon and into Friday\nevening. Unfortunately, another moisture starved system, \nresulting in minimal precipitation. \n\nHigh pressure returns through the weekend, keeping the pattern\ndry. Models are slowing a low pressure system to our west, now\nprojecting primary and secondary FROPA to cross our CWA some \nWednesday into Thursday. PWATs are 0.50-1.25\", along with \nrelatively dry RHs at the surface the precipitation would need \nto overcome. Overall, again, no improvement to drought \nconditions are expected with these showers. \n\nOtherwise, the dry conditons is bad news for the ongoing water\nresources drought impacting the state. Much of Northern Maine, \nCentral Highlands and Eastern Downeast Maine are experiencing \nonly 50-75% of normal precipitation over the last 30 days. While\nin the last 14 days areawide is less than 90% of normal. \nOverall, 30 day Standardized Precipitation Index blend has the \nNorthern 2/3rd of the CWA in the Moderate to Severe Dryness \ncategory, with portions of the Upper St. John Basin now dipping\ninto the Extreme Dryness category. \n\nThere is 38 USGS streamflow gages across the CWA which remain \ndismal, with 26 out of the 38 ranked Much Below Normal (<10th \npercentile). USGS Groundwater sites continue to show struggling \ngroundwater conditions, Clayton Lake is at Record Low (deep) \nconditions on record (35 year POR). Millinocket, Kenduskeag, \nHadley Lakes and Calais all reporting Below to Much Below Normal\nconditions. Fort Kent is very low end of Normal conditions \napproaching slipping down into Below Normal conditions. \nTherefore, the US Drought Monitor continues the entire CWA in at\nleast Moderate Drought with 58% of the CWA in Severe Drought, \nthis has the potential to worsen through the weekend with continued\nprecipitation deficits.",
